-- Singapore Penal Code 1871 - Chapter 2 (General explanations, ss 6-52B),
-- the PUNISHMENT record every punishing section fills, and the FORM OF A
-- CHARGE prescribed by the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 (ss 123-126).
--
-- Every offence module IMPORTs this one (and pc-domain). It holds what they
-- share and nothing that belongs to any one of them.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- How the general explanations reach an offence
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- s 7: "Every expression which is explained in any part of this Code is used in
-- every part of this Code in conformity with the explanation." So "dishonestly"
-- in s 378 means s 24, and CPC s 126 says the same of the words of a charge.
--
-- An offence's facts record carries such a word as ONE FLAT BOOLEAN leaf named
-- in the Code's word (`dishonestly`, `voluntarily`, `movable property`), whose
-- @desc cites the section here. The ladder here is exported as its drill-down
-- (the charge generator's DEFINITION_LADDERS). They are not nested, because a
-- call to another rule is drawn as ONE box on the caller's ladder, which the
-- reader cannot toggle in place. That is the reference row's design call,
-- carried over unchanged; notes/PLAN.md §3 states it as the convention.
--
-- Names: every definition here is `<the Code's word> within section N`, the
-- reference row's spelling, which the charge generator already calls for
-- ss 24 and 25.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pc-domain`

§ `Penal Code 1871 - Chapter 2, general explanations`

-- s 6.  Every definition of an offence, every penal provision and every
--       illustration is understood subject to the General Exceptions
--       (Chapter 4) and the Right of Private Defence (Chapter 4A), though they
--       are not repeated. - Encoded as a CONVENTION, not a rule: no offence
--       ladder repeats an exception; the exceptions module exports its own
--       ladders, and the charge sheet reads both (notes/PLAN.md §5).
-- s 6A. The definitions in ss 22A-26H (except ss 24, 25) apply to any offence
--       in any written law unless it defines the word itself. Inert here: this
--       encoding covers only this Code.
-- s 7.  One expression, one meaning, throughout. Inert: it is why one ladder
--       per word suffices.
-- s 8.  "he" is used of any person, male or female. Inert; see `Pronoun`.
-- s 9.  Singular includes plural and plural singular, unless the context
--       says otherwise. Inert.
-- [There are no sections 13 to 16, and no section 18.]

§§ `s 10 - "man" and "woman"`

-- 10. The word "man" denotes a male human being of any age; "woman" denotes a
--     female human being of any age.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 10
GIVEN s IS A Sex @desc The person's sex
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `man within section 10` s IF s EQUALS male

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 10
GIVEN s IS A Sex @desc The person's sex
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `woman within section 10` s IF s EQUALS female

-- s 11 "person" - carried by `Kind of person` in pc-domain: every kind is a
--      person. s 12 "public" includes any class of the public or any
--      community. s 17 "Government" includes any person lawfully performing
--      executive functions of the Government under any law. Both inert: an
--      offence that uses the word carries it as a leaf whose @desc quotes this.

§§ `s 19 - "judge"`

-- 19. The word "judge" denotes not only every person who is officially
--     designated as a judge, but also every person who is empowered by law to
--     give, in any legal proceeding, civil or criminal, a definitive judgment,
--     or a judgment which, if not appealed against, would be definitive, or a
--     judgment which, if confirmed by some other authority, would be
--     definitive, or who is one of a body of persons, which body of persons is
--     empowered by law to give such a judgment.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 19 - the operative atoms
DECLARE `Judge Facts` HAS
    `officially designated as a judge`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person officially designated as a judge?
    `empowered by law to give a definitive judgment in a legal proceeding`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person empowered by law to give, in any civil or criminal legal proceeding, a definitive judgment?
    `empowered by law to give a judgment which, if not appealed against, would be definitive`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc ... or a judgment which, if not appealed against, would be definitive?
    `empowered by law to give a judgment which, if confirmed by some other authority, would be definitive`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc ... or a judgment which, if confirmed by some other authority, would be definitive?
    `one of a body of persons empowered by law to give such a judgment`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the person one of a body of persons which is empowered by law to give such a judgment?

@export Whether a person is a "judge" within section 19 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN j IS A `Judge Facts` @desc The person's designation and powers of judgment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `judge within section 19` j IF
        "The word 'judge' denotes not only every person who is"
    ..  j's `officially designated as a judge`
    OR  "but also every person who is"
        ... (    j's `empowered by law to give a definitive judgment in a legal proceeding`
             OR  j's `empowered by law to give a judgment which, if not appealed against, would be definitive`
             OR  j's `empowered by law to give a judgment which, if confirmed by some other authority, would be definitive` )
    OR  j's `one of a body of persons empowered by law to give such a judgment`

§§ `s 20 - "court of justice"`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 20 - the operative atoms
DECLARE `Court Facts` HAS
    `a judge empowered by law to act judicially alone`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a judge empowered by law to act judicially alone?
    `a body of judges empowered by law to act judicially as a body`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a body of judges empowered by law to act judicially as a body?
    `acting judicially`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was that judge or body of judges acting judicially at the time?

@export Whether a tribunal is a "court of justice" within section 20 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN c IS A `Court Facts` @desc Who sits, and whether they were acting judicially
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `court of justice within section 20` c IF
        "The words 'court of justice' denote"
    AND (    c's `a judge empowered by law to act judicially alone`
         OR  c's `a body of judges empowered by law to act judicially as a body` )
    AND "when such judge or body of judges is"
    AND c's `acting judicially`

§§ `s 21 - "public servant"`

-- s 21(1)(a)-(j); Explanations 1 and 2 are why no atom asks how the person was
-- appointed or whether the appointment was valid. The atoms are `Office`'s, in
-- pc-domain.
@export Whether a person is a "public servant" within section 21(1) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN o IS AN Office @desc The office the person holds, and the duties it carries
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `public servant within section 21` o IF
        "(1) The words 'public servant' denote a person falling under any of the following descriptions:"
    ..  o's `officer in the Singapore Armed Forces`
    OR  o's `is a judge`
    OR  o's `officer of a court of justice with a judicial duty`
    OR  o's `assessor assisting a court of justice or public servant`
    OR  o's `arbitrator or referee of a court or competent public authority`
    OR  o's `holds an office empowering him to place or keep a person in confinement`
    OR  (    o's `officer of the Government or of a statutory body for a public function`
         AND (    o's `duty of maintaining law and order`
              OR  o's `duty of preserving the public peace`
              OR  o's `duty of preventing and detecting offences`
              OR  o's `duty of apprehending offenders or bringing them to justice`
              OR  o's `duty of executing legal processes`
              OR  o's `duty of maintaining order in the premises of the courts`
              OR  o's `duty of escorting and guarding prisoners and accused persons in remand`
              OR  o's `duty of protecting public health or safety or preventing public inconvenience` ) )
    OR  o's `officer with a duty touching the property or pecuniary interests of Government, or in the service or pay of Government, or remunerated for a public duty`
    OR  o's `member of the Public Service, Judicial Service or Legal Service Commission`
    OR  (    o's `employed to carry out a duty in paragraph (g)(i) to (vii) on behalf of the Government or a statutory body`
         AND "when that person is"
         AND o's `performing such duties at the time` )

-- s 21(2): for ss 175, 178, 179, 180 and 228 only.
@export Whether a person is a "public servant" as the words are used in sections 175, 178, 179, 180 and 228 of the Penal Code 1871 (s 21(2))
GIVEN o IS AN Office @desc The office the person holds, and the duties it carries
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `public servant within section 21, for sections 175, 178, 179, 180 and 228` o IF
        `public servant within section 21` o
    AND "(2) Despite subsection (1), ... does not include"
        ... NOT o's `a judge as defined in the Administration of Justice (Protection) Act 2016`

§§ `s 22 - "property"`

-- 22. "immovable property" means land, benefits to arise out of land and
--     things attached to the earth or permanently fastened to anything
--     attached to the earth; "movable property" includes property of every
--     description, except immovable property; "property" means money and all
--     other property, movable or immovable, including things in action, other
--     intangible or incorporeal property and virtual currency; "virtual
--     currency" means a digital representation of value ... that can be
--     digitally traded and functions as a medium of exchange, a unit of account
--     or store of value, regardless of whether it is legal tender ...
--     Illustration: Writings, relating to real or personal property or rights,
--     are movable property.
--
-- "property" itself is inclusive ("money and all other property ... including")
-- and has no closed test to encode: every thing the Code calls property is.
-- The two definitions with a test are encoded.
@export Whether property is "immovable property" within section 22 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN p IS A `Property` @desc The thing, and how it stands to the land
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `immovable property within section 22` p IF
        "'immovable property' means"
    ..  p's `is land`
    OR  p's `is a benefit to arise out of land`
    OR  p's `is a thing attached to the earth`
    OR  p's `is permanently fastened to anything attached to the earth`

@export Whether property is "movable property" within section 22 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN p IS A `Property` @desc The thing, and how it stands to the land
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `movable property within section 22` p IF
        "'movable property' includes property of every description, except immovable property"
    ..  NOT `immovable property within section 22` p

@export Whether property is "virtual currency" within section 22 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN p IS A `Property` @desc The thing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `virtual currency within section 22` p IF
        "'virtual currency' means"
    AND p's `is a digital representation of value in money or money's worth`
    AND "that"
        ... p's `can be digitally traded`
    AND "and"
        ... p's `functions as a medium of exchange, a unit of account or store of value`

-- s 22A. "Fault element" is any state of mind, proof of which is needed to
--     establish liability (including intention, wilfulness, knowledge,
--     rashness and negligence); "physical element" is any other fact proof of
--     which is needed. Inert: it names the two kinds of leaf every offence
--     ladder has. notes/PLAN.md §3 asks encoders to say, in each leaf's @desc,
--     which kind it is where that matters (s 26G, s 26H).

§§ `s 23 - wrongful gain and wrongful loss`

-- Explanations 1-3 (retaining as well as acquiring; kept out as well as
-- deprived; gain by keeping, loss by not getting) widen the atoms, and ride in
-- the @desc.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 23 - the operative atoms of subsections (1) and (2)
DECLARE `Gain or Loss Facts` HAS
    `gain by unlawful means of property to which the person gaining it is not legally entitled`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person gain, by unlawful means, property they were not legally entitled to? Gain includes keeping what one has, and retaining wrongfully as well as acquiring (Explanations 1, 2).
    `avoidance by unlawful means of a loss of property which the person is not legally entitled to avoid`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person avoid, by unlawful means, a loss of property they were not legally entitled to avoid?
    `loss by unlawful means of property to which the person losing it is legally entitled`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person lose, by unlawful means, property they were legally entitled to? Loss includes being kept out of property, and not getting what one might get (Explanations 1, 3).
    `exposure of risk to a loss by unlawful means of property to which the person exposed is legally entitled`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the person exposed, by unlawful means, to the risk of losing property they were legally entitled to?

@export Whether there is a "wrongful gain" within section 23(1) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN g IS A `Gain or Loss Facts` @desc What was gained, avoided, lost or put at risk, and by what means
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `wrongful gain within section 23` g IF
        "(1) A 'wrongful gain' is"
    ..  g's `gain by unlawful means of property to which the person gaining it is not legally entitled`
    OR  g's `avoidance by unlawful means of a loss of property which the person is not legally entitled to avoid`

@export Whether there is a "wrongful loss" within section 23(2) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN g IS A `Gain or Loss Facts` @desc What was gained, avoided, lost or put at risk, and by what means
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `wrongful loss within section 23` g IF
        "(2) A 'wrongful loss' is"
    ..  g's `loss by unlawful means of property to which the person losing it is legally entitled`
    OR  g's `exposure of risk to a loss by unlawful means of property to which the person exposed is legally entitled`

§§ `s 24 - dishonestly`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 24 - the operative atoms of paragraphs (a) and (b)
DECLARE `Dishonesty Facts` HAS
    `intention of causing wrongful gain to A or another person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused do the act intending to cause a wrongful gain (s 23) to themselves or to anyone else, whether temporary or permanent?
    `intention of causing wrongful loss to another person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused do the act intending to cause a wrongful loss (s 23) to another person, whether temporary or permanent?
    `dishonest by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would reasonable and honest people regard the act as dishonest?
    `A knows that that act is dishonest by such standards`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused know that reasonable and honest people would regard the act as dishonest?

@export Whether an act was done "dishonestly" within section 24 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN d IS A `Dishonesty Facts` @desc What the accused intended by the act, and what the accused knew about it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `dishonestly within section 24` d IF
        "A person (A) is said to do an act dishonestly if -"
    ..  "(a) A does that act with the"
        ...  (    d's `intention of causing wrongful gain to A or another person`
              OR  d's `intention of causing wrongful loss to another person` )
        ...  ", regardless of whether such gain or loss is temporary or permanent"
    OR  "(b) that act done by A is"
        ...  d's `dishonest by the ordinary standards of reasonable and honest persons`
        AND  d's `A knows that that act is dishonest by such standards`

§§ `s 25 - fraudulently`

-- Explanation 2 ("sufficient in any charge ... to allege a general intent to act
-- fraudulently without naming any particular person intended to be deceived")
-- is a rule of pleading: a charge builder need not name the person deceived.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 25 - the operative atoms, with Explanation 1 as a proviso
DECLARE `Fraud Facts` HAS
    `intent to deceive another person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused do the act intending to deceive someone?
    `an advantage should accrue to A or another person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the deception meant to bring an advantage to the accused or to anyone else?
    `detriment should befall B or another person other than A`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the deception meant to bring a detriment on the person deceived or on anyone other than the accused?
    `the advantage or detriment intended is so slight that no reasonable person would complain of it`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the advantage or detriment intended so slight that no reasonable person of ordinary sense or temper would complain of it? (Explanation 1 - if so, the act is not done fraudulently.)

@export Whether an act was done "fraudulently" within section 25 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN f IS A `Fraud Facts` @desc What the accused intended by the deception
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `fraudulently within section 25` f IF
        "A person (A) is said to do an act fraudulently if A does that act with"
    ... f's `intent to deceive another person`
    ... "and by means of such deception, that"
    ... (    f's `an advantage should accrue to A or another person`
         OR  f's `detriment should befall B or another person other than A` )
    ... ", regardless of whether such advantage or detriment is temporary or permanent."
    ... "Explanation 1.-"
    ... NOT (f's `the advantage or detriment intended is so slight that no reasonable person would complain of it`)

§§ `s 26 - "reason to believe"`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 26
DECLARE `Belief Facts` HAS
    `sufficient cause to believe that thing`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person have sufficient cause to believe the thing in question?

@export Whether a person has "reason to believe" a thing within section 26 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN b IS A `Belief Facts` @desc What cause the person had to believe the thing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reason to believe within section 26` b IF
        "A person is said to have 'reason to believe' a thing, if he has"
    ..  b's `sufficient cause to believe that thing`
    ..  ", but not otherwise."

§§ `s 26A - "voluntarily"`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 26A - the operative atoms
DECLARE `Voluntariness Facts` HAS
    `caused it by means whereby he intended to cause it`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person cause the effect by means by which he intended to cause it?
    `knew the means to be likely to cause it when employing them`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc When employing the means, did the person know them to be likely to cause the effect?
    `had reason to believe the means to be likely to cause it when employing them`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc When employing the means, did the person have reason to believe (s 26) them to be likely to cause the effect?

@export Whether a person caused an effect "voluntarily" within section 26A of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN v IS A `Voluntariness Facts` @desc By what means the effect was caused, and what the person intended, knew or had reason to believe of them
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `voluntarily within section 26A` v IF
        "A person is said to cause an effect 'voluntarily' when he causes it"
    ..  v's `caused it by means whereby he intended to cause it`
    OR  "by means which, at the time of employing those means, he"
        ... (    v's `knew the means to be likely to cause it when employing them`
             OR  v's `had reason to believe the means to be likely to cause it when employing them` )

§§ `s 26B - "good faith"`

-- 26B. Nothing is said to be done or believed in good faith which is done or
--      believed without due care and attention.
--
-- The Code states only the negative. This ladder reads it as the whole test -
-- done with due care and attention, therefore in good faith - which is FORK
-- G-3 in notes/general-forks.md. An encoder whose section needs more (honesty
-- as well as care) adds that leaf beside this one, not inside it.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 26B
DECLARE `Good Faith Facts` HAS
    `done or believed with due care and attention`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the thing done or believed with due care and attention?

@export Whether a thing is done or believed "in good faith" within section 26B of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN g IS A `Good Faith Facts` @desc Whether due care and attention was taken
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `in good faith within section 26B` g IF
        "Nothing is said to be done or believed in good faith which is done or believed without"
    ..  g's `done or believed with due care and attention`

§§ `ss 26C-26F - intentionally, knowingly, rashly, negligently`

-- 26C(3)-(4) and its Explanation (natural and probable consequence alone is
-- not intention; foresight may ground an inference; intention is not motive)
-- are rules about proof and meaning, not atoms. They ride here as text.
@ref Penal Code 1871 ss 26C, 26D, 26E, 26F - the operative atoms
DECLARE `Fault Facts` HAS
    -- s 26C
    `did the act deliberately`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person do the act deliberately? (s 26C(1))
    `with the purpose of causing that effect`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person do the thing with the purpose of causing the effect? (s 26C(2)(a)) Motive and desire are beside the point (Explanation).
    `knowing that that effect would be virtually certain to result`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person know the effect would be virtually certain (barring an unforeseen intervention) to result? (s 26C(2)(b))
    -- s 26D
    `aware that the circumstance exists, will exist or is virtually certain to exist`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person act aware that the circumstance existed, would exist, or was virtually certain (barring an unforeseen intervention) to exist? (s 26D(1))
    `aware that the effect will be caused or is virtually certain to be caused`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person act aware that the effect would be caused, or was virtually certain (barring an unforeseen intervention) to be caused? (s 26D(2))
    -- s 26E
    `knowing that there is a real risk that the circumstance exists or will exist`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person act knowing there was a real risk that the circumstance existed or would exist? (s 26E(1))
    `knowing that there is a real risk that the effect will be caused`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person act knowing there was a real risk that the effect would be caused? (s 26E(2))
    `it would have been unreasonable to have taken that risk`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would it have been unreasonable to take that risk? (s 26E(1), (2))
    -- s 26F
    `omits to do an act which a reasonable person would do`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person omit to do something a reasonable person would do? (s 26F(1))
    `does an act which a reasonable person would not do`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person do something a reasonable person would not do? (s 26F(1))

@export Whether a person does an act "intentionally" within section 26C(1) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN m IS A `Fault Facts` @desc The person's state of mind
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `does the act intentionally within section 26C(1)` m IF
        "(1) A person is said to do an act intentionally where that person does an act"
    ..  m's `did the act deliberately`

@export Whether a person causes an effect "intentionally" within section 26C(2) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN m IS A `Fault Facts` @desc The person's state of mind
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `causes the effect intentionally within section 26C(2)` m IF
        "(2) A person is said to cause an effect intentionally where that person does anything that causes an effect -"
    ..  "(a)"
        ... m's `with the purpose of causing that effect`
    OR  "(b)"
        ... m's `knowing that that effect would be virtually certain to result`

@export Whether a person does an act "knowingly" in respect of a circumstance within section 26D(1) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN m IS A `Fault Facts` @desc The person's state of mind
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `knowingly in respect of a circumstance within section 26D(1)` m IF
        "(1) Whoever does an act with"
    ..  m's `aware that the circumstance exists, will exist or is virtually certain to exist`

@export Whether a person does an act "knowingly" in respect of an effect within section 26D(2) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN m IS A `Fault Facts` @desc The person's state of mind
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `knowingly in respect of an effect within section 26D(2)` m IF
        "(2) Whoever does an act with"
    ..  m's `aware that the effect will be caused or is virtually certain to be caused`

@export Whether a person does an act "rashly" in respect of a circumstance within section 26E(1) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN m IS A `Fault Facts` @desc The person's state of mind
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rashly in respect of a circumstance within section 26E(1)` m IF
        "(1) Whoever does any act"
    AND m's `knowing that there is a real risk that the circumstance exists or will exist`
    AND "is said to do that act rashly ..., if"
        ... m's `it would have been unreasonable to have taken that risk`

@export Whether a person does an act "rashly" in respect of an effect within section 26E(2) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN m IS A `Fault Facts` @desc The person's state of mind
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `rashly in respect of an effect within section 26E(2)` m IF
        "(2) Whoever does any act"
    AND m's `knowing that there is a real risk that the effect will be caused`
    AND "is said to do that act rashly ..., if"
        ... m's `it would have been unreasonable to have taken that risk`

@export Whether a person acts "negligently" within section 26F(1) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN m IS A `Fault Facts` @desc What the person did or omitted, measured against a reasonable person
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `negligently within section 26F` m IF
        "(1) Whoever"
    ..  m's `omits to do an act which a reasonable person would do`
    OR  m's `does an act which a reasonable person would not do`
    ..  "is said to do so negligently."

-- The ladder of fault: s 26D(3), s 26E(3), s 26F(2). Where a section requires
-- one fault word, which proven states of mind establish it. Read literally -
-- each subsection lists its own substitutes and they are not chained (FORK G-4).
@export Whether a fault element the offence requires is established by the state of mind proven (ss 26D(3), 26E(3), 26F(2) of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN required IS A `Fault word` @desc The fault word the offence uses
      proven   IS A `Fault word` @desc The state of mind proven
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the fault element` required `is established by` proven IF
    CONSIDER required
    WHEN intentionally           THEN proven EQUALS intentionally
    WHEN knowingly               THEN proven EQUALS knowingly OR proven EQUALS intentionally OR proven EQUALS `with wilful blindness`
    WHEN `with wilful blindness` THEN proven EQUALS `with wilful blindness`
    WHEN rashly                  THEN proven EQUALS rashly OR proven EQUALS intentionally OR proven EQUALS knowingly
    WHEN negligently             THEN proven EQUALS negligently OR proven EQUALS intentionally OR proven EQUALS knowingly OR proven EQUALS rashly

§§ `s 26G - transferred fault`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 26G - the operative atoms of subsections (1)-(3)
DECLARE `Transferred Fault Facts` HAS
    `a fault element of the offence is intention or knowledge`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a fault element of the offence intention or knowledge? (s 26G(1))
    `section 301(1) applies to the offence`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does s 301(1) (culpable homicide of a person other than the one intended) apply? If so s 26G does not. (s 26G(1))
    `all the fault elements of the offence are proven`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are all the fault elements of the offence proven against the accused? (s 26G(2); s 26G(4) - nothing here relaxes this)
    `all the physical elements of the offence are proven`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are all the physical elements of the offence proven against the accused? (s 26G(2))
    `a defence or exception applies`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a defence or exception apply? (s 26G(2) "assuming no defence or exception applies"; s 26G(5) - judged as though the act concerned the intended person or thing)
    `the physical elements included an act or effect concerning a person or thing`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the physical elements include doing an act or causing an effect concerning a person or thing? (s 26G(2)(a))
    `the accused believed or intended that it would concern a different person or thing`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time, did the accused believe or intend that the act or effect would concern a different person or thing? (s 26G(2)(b))
    `done negligently in respect of the person or thing actually concerned`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused do or cause those physical elements negligently (s 26F) in respect of the person or thing actually concerned? (s 26G(3))

@export Whether the accused is guilty despite the act or effect concerning a person or thing other than the one intended, under section 26G of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN t IS A `Transferred Fault Facts` @desc The offence's elements, and whom or what the accused meant the act to concern
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `guilty despite transferred fault within section 26G` t IF
        "(1) This section applies to any offence ... where a fault element of the offence is"
    ... t's `a fault element of the offence is intention or knowledge`
    ... "except where"
    ... NOT t's `section 301(1) applies to the offence`
    AND "(2) Where"
        ... t's `all the fault elements of the offence are proven`
        ... t's `all the physical elements of the offence are proven`
    AND "and assuming no"
        ... NOT t's `a defence or exception applies`
    AND "despite the following circumstances: (a)"
        ... t's `the physical elements included an act or effect concerning a person or thing`
        ... "(b)"
        ... t's `the accused believed or intended that it would concern a different person or thing`
    AND "(3) Subsection (2) applies only where the accused person did or caused the physical elements"
        ... t's `done negligently in respect of the person or thing actually concerned`

§§ `s 26H - strict liability`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 26H - the operative atoms of subsections (1) and (4)
DECLARE `Strict Liability Facts` HAS
    `for every physical element of the offence there is no corresponding fault element`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the offence lack a corresponding fault element for every one of its physical elements, whether or not any law calls it strict? (s 26H(1), (3))
    `exercised reasonable care in committing all the acts or omissions that are physical elements`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the accused prove that in committing every act or omission that is a physical element of the offence, he exercised reasonable care? (s 26H(4))

@export Whether an offence is "an offence of strict liability" within section 26H(1) of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN s IS A `Strict Liability Facts` @desc The offence's elements
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `strict liability offence within section 26H(1)` s IF
        "(1) An offence of strict liability ... is one where,"
    ..  s's `for every physical element of the offence there is no corresponding fault element`

@export Whether the defence of reasonable care in section 26H(4) of the Penal Code 1871 is made out
GIVEN s IS A `Strict Liability Facts` @desc The offence's elements, and the care the accused took
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `defence of reasonable care within section 26H(4)` s IF
        "(4) It is a defence for any person charged with"
    AND `strict liability offence within section 26H(1)` s
    AND "to prove that in committing all the acts or omissions that are physical elements of the offence, he"
        ... s's `exercised reasonable care in committing all the acts or omissions that are physical elements`

§§ `s 27 - property in possession of spouse, clerk or servant`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 27 - the operative atoms, with the Explanation
DECLARE `Possession Facts` HAS
    `in the possession of the person's spouse`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the property in the possession of the person's spouse?
    `in the possession of the person's clerk or servant`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the property in the possession of the person's clerk or servant - including one employed temporarily or on a particular occasion in that capacity (Explanation)?
    `on account of that person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it held on account of that person?

@export Whether property is in a person's possession by reason of section 27 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN p IS A `Possession Facts` @desc Who holds the property, and on whose account
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `in that person's possession within section 27` p IF
        "When property is in the possession of a person's"
    AND (    p's `in the possession of the person's spouse`
         OR  p's `in the possession of the person's clerk or servant` )
    AND p's `on account of that person`
    AND ", it is in that person's possession within the meaning of this Code."

§§ `s 28 - "counterfeit"`

-- Explanation 1 (the imitation need not be exact) widens the first atom.
-- Explanation 2 is a presumption: where the resemblance is such that a person
-- might be deceived, the intent or knowledge is PRESUMED until the contrary is
-- proved. Encoded as a third route to the mental element, closed by proof of
-- the contrary.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 28 - the operative atoms, with Explanation 2 as a rebuttable presumption
DECLARE `Counterfeiting Facts` HAS
    `causes one thing to resemble another thing`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person cause one thing to resemble another? The imitation need not be exact (Explanation 1).
    `intending by means of that resemblance to practise deception`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person intend by the resemblance to practise deception?
    `knowing it to be likely that deception will thereby be practised`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person know it was likely that deception would be practised by it?
    `the resemblance is such that a person might be deceived thereby`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the resemblance such that a person might be deceived by it? (Explanation 2)
    `the contrary is proved`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the person proved that he neither intended deception nor knew it likely? (Explanation 2)

@export Whether a person "counterfeits" within section 28 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN c IS A `Counterfeiting Facts` @desc The resemblance, and what the person intended or knew by it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `counterfeits within section 28` c IF
        "A person is said to 'counterfeit' who"
    AND c's `causes one thing to resemble another thing`
    AND (    c's `intending by means of that resemblance to practise deception`
         OR  c's `knowing it to be likely that deception will thereby be practised`
         OR  (    "Explanation 2.-"
              ... c's `the resemblance is such that a person might be deceived thereby`
              ... "it shall be presumed until"
              ... NOT c's `the contrary is proved` ) )

§§ `ss 29-31A - document, writing, electronic record, valuable security, will, instrument`

-- s 29A "writing" includes any mode of representing or reproducing words,
-- figures, drawings or symbols in a visible form - carried in the @desc of
-- `is a document in writing`. s 29B "electronic record" has the meaning in the
-- Electronic Transactions Act 2010, which is not encoded: a leaf.
@export Whether a thing is a "document" within section 29 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN d IS A Document @desc The thing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `document within section 29` d IF
        "The word 'document' includes, in addition to a document in writing -"
    ..  d's `is a document in writing`
    OR  d's `is a map, plan, graph or drawing`
    OR  d's `is a photograph`
    OR  d's `is a label, marking or other writing identifying or describing a thing it forms part of or is attached to`
    OR  d's `is a device embodying sounds or other data capable of being reproduced`
    OR  d's `is a device embodying visual images capable of being reproduced`
    OR  d's `is material bearing marks having a meaning for persons qualified to interpret them`

@export Whether a thing is a "valuable security" within section 30 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN d IS A Document @desc The thing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `valuable security within section 30` d IF
        (     "(1) The words 'valuable security' denote"
          AND (    `document within section 29` d
               OR  d's `is an electronic record` )
          AND "which is, or purports to be, a document or an electronic record"
          AND (    d's `is or purports to be a document whereby a legal right is created, extended, transferred, restricted, extinguished or released`
               OR  d's `is or purports to be a document whereby a person acknowledges legal liability or that he has not a certain legal right` ) )
    OR  (     "(2) ... includes"
          AND d's `is a card which has money or money's worth or other financial rights attached` )

@export Whether a thing is "a will" within section 31 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN d IS A Document @desc The thing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a will within section 31` d IF
        "The words 'a will' denote any"
    AND d's `is testamentary`
    AND `document within section 29` d

-- s 31A, for Chapters 12 and 18 only. "die" is carried in the @desc of
-- `is a seal or die`.
@export Whether a thing is an "instrument" within section 31A of the Penal Code 1871 (Chapters 12 and 18)
GIVEN d IS A Document @desc The thing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `instrument within section 31A` d IF
        "'instrument' includes"
    ..  `document within section 29` d
    OR  d's `is a postage stamp or revenue stamp`
    OR  d's `is a seal or die`
    OR  d's `is a device on or in which information is recorded or stored`

-- s 32. Words which refer to acts done extend also to illegal omissions,
--     except where a contrary intention appears. s 33. "act" and "omission"
--     include a series; (2) a series of acts one of which caused an effect
--     causes it, though it is not known which. Both inert: they widen every
--     offence's act-leaf, and an encoder's @desc says so where it matters.

§§ `s 34 - acts done by several persons in furtherance of common intention`

-- Carried in a charge by `Particulars`'s `common intention`, which adds the
-- s 34 rider to the closing words. This ladder is the drill-down.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 34
DECLARE `Common Intention Facts` HAS
    `a criminal act is done by several persons`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the criminal act done by several persons?
    `in furtherance of the common intention of all`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it done in furtherance of the common intention of all of them?

@export Whether each of several persons is liable for an act as if done by him alone, under section 34 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN c IS A `Common Intention Facts` @desc Who did the act, and with what shared intention
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `each is liable as if he alone did it within section 34` c IF
        "When"
    AND c's `a criminal act is done by several persons`
    AND c's `in furtherance of the common intention of all`
    AND ", each of such persons is liable for that act in the same manner as if the act were done by him alone."

§§ `s 35 - act criminal by reason of criminal knowledge or intention`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 35
DECLARE `Joint Knowledge Facts` HAS
    `the act is criminal only by reason of its being done with a criminal knowledge or intention`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the act criminal only because it is done with a criminal knowledge or intention?
    `the act is done by several persons`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it done by several persons?
    `this person joins in the act with such knowledge or intention`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did this person join in the act with that knowledge or intention?

@export Whether a person who joins in an act with criminal knowledge or intention is liable for it under section 35 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN j IS A `Joint Knowledge Facts` @desc The act, and this person's part in it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `liable for the act within section 35` j IF
        j's `the act is criminal only by reason of its being done with a criminal knowledge or intention`
    AND j's `the act is done by several persons`
    AND j's `this person joins in the act with such knowledge or intention`

-- s 36. Causing an effect partly by act and partly by omission is the same
--     offence as causing it by either. Inert: it widens the act-leaf.
--     Illustration: A intentionally causes Z's death partly by illegally
--     omitting to give Z food and partly by beating Z. A has committed murder.

§§ `s 37 - cooperation by doing one of several acts`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 37
DECLARE `Cooperation Facts` HAS
    `the offence is committed by means of several acts`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the offence committed by means of several acts?
    `intentionally cooperates in the commission of that offence`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did this person intentionally cooperate in committing it?
    `by doing any one of those acts, singly or jointly with any other person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did this person do any one of those acts, alone or jointly with another?

@export Whether a person who does one of several acts constituting an offence commits it, under section 37 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN c IS A `Cooperation Facts` @desc The acts, and this person's part in them
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `commits that offence within section 37` c IF
        "When"
    AND c's `the offence is committed by means of several acts`
    AND "whoever"
        ... c's `intentionally cooperates in the commission of that offence`
        ... c's `by doing any one of those acts, singly or jointly with any other person`
    AND "commits that offence."

-- s 38. Several persons engaged in one criminal act may be guilty of
--     different offences by means of it. Inert: each person's facts are read
--     against the offence separately, which is what the encoding does anyway.
-- s 39. [Repealed by Act 15 of 2019]

§§ `s 40 - "offence"`

-- 40.-(1) Except in the Chapters and sections mentioned in subsections (2)
--     and (3), "offence" denotes a thing made punishable by this Code.
--     (2) In Chapters 4, 4A, 5 and 5A, and in sections 4, 187, 194, 195, 203,
--     204B, 211, 213, 214, 221, 222, 223, 224, 225, 327, 328, 329, 330, 331,
--     347, 348, 388 and 389, "offence" denotes a thing punishable under this
--     Code or under any other written law for the time being in force.
--     (3) In sections 141, 176, 177, 201, 202, 212, 216 and 441, "offence" has
--     the same meaning when the thing punishable under any other law ... is
--     punishable under such law with imprisonment for a term of 6 months or
--     upwards, whether with or without fine.
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 40
DECLARE `Offence Facts` HAS
    `made punishable by this Code`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the thing made punishable by the Penal Code 1871?
    `punishable under any other written law for the time being in force`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it punishable under any other written law in force?
    `punishable under that other law with imprisonment for a term of 6 months or upwards`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it punishable under that other law with imprisonment for 6 months or upwards, with or without fine? (s 41: a maximum of exactly 6 months counts.)

GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the Chapters of section 40(2)` MEANS LIST "4", "4A", "5", "5A"

GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the sections of section 40(2)` MEANS
    LIST "4", "187", "194", "195", "203", "204B", "211", "213", "214", "221", "222", "223", "224", "225", "327", "328", "329", "330", "331", "347", "348", "388", "389"

GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`the sections of section 40(3)` MEANS
    LIST "141", "176", "177", "201", "202", "212", "216", "441"

@export Whether a thing is an "offence" as the word is used in a given Chapter and section of the Penal Code 1871 (s 40)
GIVEN chapter IS A STRING @desc The Chapter in which the word "offence" is read - for example "4A"
      section IS A STRING @desc The section in which the word "offence" is read - for example "187"
      o       IS AN `Offence Facts` @desc Under which law the thing is punishable, and how severely
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `offence within section 40` chapter section o IF
    BRANCH
        IF   elem chapter `the Chapters of section 40(2)` OR elem section `the sections of section 40(2)`
        THEN o's `made punishable by this Code` OR o's `punishable under any other written law for the time being in force`
        IF   elem section `the sections of section 40(3)`
        THEN o's `made punishable by this Code`
             OR (o's `punishable under any other written law for the time being in force` AND o's `punishable under that other law with imprisonment for a term of 6 months or upwards`)
        OTHERWISE o's `made punishable by this Code`

-- s 41 is encoded under the Punishment record below, which it reads.
--
-- s 42 "obscene" - any thing or matter whose effect, taken as a whole, tends to
--     deprave and corrupt persons likely, in all the circumstances, to read,
--     see or hear it. One atom; carried as a leaf by Chapter 14's encoder.
-- s 43 "illegal", "unlawful", "legally bound to do" - encoded below.
-- s 44 "injury" - encoded below.
-- s 44A "bodily injury" includes a series of bodily injuries. Inert.
-- s 45 "life", s 46 "death" - of a human being unless the context says
--     otherwise. s 47 "animal" - any living creature other than a human being.
--     s 48 "vessel" - anything made for the conveyance by water of human
--     beings or property. s 49 "year" and "month" - Gregorian calendar (so
--     daydate's calendar is the Code's). s 50 "section" - a numbered portion
--     of a Chapter. s 51 "oath" - includes a solemn affirmation and any
--     declaration required or authorised by law before a public servant or for
--     proof. All inert: each is carried in the @desc of the leaf that uses it.
-- s 52. [Repealed by Act 15 of 2019]

§§ `s 43 - "illegal", "unlawful" and "legally bound to do"`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 43
DECLARE `Unlawfulness Facts` HAS
    `is an offence`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the thing an offence?
    `is prohibited by law`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it prohibited by law?
    `furnishes ground for a civil action`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it furnish ground for a civil action?

@export Whether a thing is "illegal" or "unlawful" within section 43 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN u IS AN `Unlawfulness Facts` @desc The thing, and what the law says of it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `illegal within section 43` u IF
        "The word 'illegal' or 'unlawful' is applicable to every thing"
    ..  u's `is an offence`
    OR  u's `is prohibited by law`
    OR  u's `furnishes ground for a civil action`

-- "a person is said to be 'legally bound to do' whatever it is illegal or
-- unlawful in him to omit" - so the same ladder, read over the OMISSION.
@export Whether a person is "legally bound to do" a thing within section 43 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN omission IS AN `Unlawfulness Facts` @desc The omission to do the thing, and what the law says of that omission
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `legally bound to do within section 43` omission IF
        "a person is said to be 'legally bound to do' whatever it is"
    ..  `illegal within section 43` omission
    ..  "in him to omit"

§§ `s 44 - "injury"`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 44
DECLARE `Injury Facts` HAS
    `harm caused to a person`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was harm of any kind caused to a person?
    `illegally caused`
        IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it caused illegally (s 43)?
    `in body`       IS A BOOLEAN @desc ... to the person's body?
    `in mind`       IS A BOOLEAN @desc ... to the person's mind?
    `in reputation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc ... to the person's reputation?
    `in property`   IS A BOOLEAN @desc ... to the person's property?

@export Whether there is an "injury" within section 44 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN i IS AN `Injury Facts` @desc The harm, how it was caused, and what it touched
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `injury within section 44` i IF
        "The word 'injury' denotes any"
    AND i's `harm caused to a person`
    AND "whatever"
        ... i's `illegally caused`
    AND "to any person, in"
        ... (    i's `in body`
             OR  i's `in mind`
             OR  i's `in reputation`
             OR  i's `in property` )

§ `Penal Code 1871 - the data of a punishment (ss 41, 53)`

-- s 53: the punishments are death, imprisonment, forfeiture of property, fine
-- and caning (with a rattan). s 54: "imprisonment for life" is for the
-- duration of the person's natural life. Chapter 3 is the general-part
-- group's to encode; this record is shared so that every punishing section
-- states its punishment the same way, and so that ss 41, 115, 116, 118, 120 and
-- 512 can read it.

-- The six kinds (s 53, with s 54's imprisonment for life split from a term)
-- are the six `How a punishment is prescribed` fields of `Punishment`.

-- How a punishing section prescribes one kind of punishment, in the Code's own
-- verbs:
--   "shall be punished with ..." (and "and with ...")        `shall be punished with`
--   "... or with fine" / "death or imprisonment for life"   `or with`
--       - one of alternatives the court chooses among
--   "shall also be liable to ..." / "and shall be liable to" `shall also be liable to`
--   the kind is not mentioned                              `not prescribed`
@ref Penal Code 1871 s 53, and the punishing words of every punishing section
DECLARE `How a punishment is prescribed` IS ONE OF
    `not prescribed`
    `shall be punished with`
    `or with`
    `shall also be liable to`

-- One record per punishing provision, filled verbatim from it. Terms are in
-- MONTHS so that "6 months" and "20 years" are both whole numbers (240). A
-- maximum or minimum the section does not state is NOTHING - never 0.
@ref Penal Code 1871 ss 53, 54 - one punishing provision's punishment, as data
DECLARE Punishment HAS
    section      IS A STRING @desc The punishing provision - for example "420(1)"
    words        IS A STRING @desc The punishment in the section's own words, verbatim - for example "imprisonment for a term which may extend to 3 years, or with fine, or with both"
    death        IS A `How a punishment is prescribed` @desc How death is prescribed, if at all
    `imprisonment for life`
                 IS A `How a punishment is prescribed` @desc How imprisonment for life is prescribed, if at all
    imprisonment IS A `How a punishment is prescribed` @desc How imprisonment for a term is prescribed, if at all
    `maximum term in months`
                 IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The term which the imprisonment "may extend to", in months; NOTHING if none is stated
    `minimum term in months`
                 IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The term the imprisonment shall be "not less than", in months; NOTHING if none is stated
    `forfeiture of property`
                 IS A `How a punishment is prescribed` @desc How forfeiture of property is prescribed, if at all
    fine         IS A `How a punishment is prescribed` @desc How a fine is prescribed, if at all
    `maximum fine`
                 IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The fine the section says "may extend to", in dollars; NOTHING if unlimited or not stated
    `minimum fine`
                 IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The fine the section says shall be "not less than", in dollars; NOTHING if none is stated
    caning       IS A `How a punishment is prescribed` @desc How caning is prescribed, if at all
    `minimum strokes`
                 IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The strokes the caning shall be "not less than"; NOTHING if none is stated
    `maximum strokes`
                 IS A MAYBE NUMBER @desc The strokes the caning "may extend to"; NOTHING if none is stated
    `or with both`
                 IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the section add "or with both" / "or to both" to its alternatives?

GIVEN h IS A `How a punishment is prescribed`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`prescribed at all` h MEANS NOT (h EQUALS `not prescribed`)

-- The words of ss 115, 118, 512(1): "punishable with death or imprisonment for life".
@export Whether an offence is "punishable with death or imprisonment for life" (as ss 115, 118 and 512(1) of the Penal Code 1871 use the words)
GIVEN p IS A Punishment @desc The punishment the offence's section prescribes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `punishable with death or imprisonment for life` p IF
        `prescribed at all` (p's death)
    OR  `prescribed at all` (p's `imprisonment for life`)

-- The words of ss 116 and 120: "punishable with imprisonment".
@export Whether an offence is "punishable with imprisonment" (as ss 116 and 120 of the Penal Code 1871 use the words)
GIVEN p IS A Punishment @desc The punishment the offence's section prescribes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `punishable with imprisonment` p IF
        `prescribed at all` (p's imprisonment)
    OR  `prescribed at all` (p's `imprisonment for life`)

-- s 512(3): "fixed by law, a specified minimum sentence or a mandatory minimum
-- sentence of imprisonment or fine or caning".
@export Whether a punishment is fixed by law or carries a minimum sentence of imprisonment, fine or caning (s 512(3) of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN p IS A Punishment @desc The punishment the offence's section prescribes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `fixed by law or carries a minimum sentence` p IF
        p's death EQUALS `shall be punished with`
    OR  p's `imprisonment for life` EQUALS `shall be punished with`
    OR  isJust (p's `minimum term in months`)
    OR  isJust (p's `minimum fine`)
    OR  isJust (p's `minimum strokes`)

§§ `s 41 - offence with specified term of imprisonment`

-- 41. An offence described ... as being punishable with imprisonment for a
--     specified term or upwards includes an offence for which the specified
--     term is the maximum term of imprisonment.
--
-- A section that prescribes imprisonment but states no maximum does not answer
-- the question, and the encoding says so rather than guessing.
@export Whether an offence is "punishable with imprisonment for a term of" the given months "or upwards", read with section 41 of the Penal Code 1871
GIVEN months IS A NUMBER     @desc The specified term, in months - for example 6
      p      IS A Punishment @desc The punishment the offence's section prescribes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `punishable with imprisonment for` months `months or upwards within section 41` p IF
    BRANCH
        IF `prescribed at all` (p's `imprisonment for life`) THEN TRUE
        IF NOT (`prescribed at all` (p's imprisonment))       THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER p's `maximum term in months`
            WHEN JUST m  THEN m AT LEAST months
            WHEN NOTHING THEN `the section states no maximum term of imprisonment, so section 41 cannot be applied`

@ref Penal Code 1871 s 41 - a section prescribing imprisonment without a stated maximum
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the section states no maximum term of imprisonment, so section 41 cannot be applied` MEANS
    REFUSE "the section states no maximum term of imprisonment, so section 41 cannot be applied"

§ `Criminal Procedure Code 2010 - the form of a charge`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Why a charge is an L4 artefact at all - CPC 2010 s 123(5)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
--
--   123.-(5) The fact that the charge is made is equivalent to a statement
--            that every legal condition required by law to constitute the
--            offence charged was fulfilled in the particular case.
--
-- So a charge is a claim that every leaf of the offence's ladder is TRUE. The
-- `Charge` record carries that claim as `made out`, and the text is built ONLY
-- when it holds. Otherwise the record carries a refusal naming the elements
-- that are missing, and no text.
--
--   123.-(4) The law and section of the law against which the offence is said
--            to have been committed must be mentioned in the charge.
--   124.-(1) particulars of time, place and person (`Particulars`, pc-domain)
--   125.     the MANNER, where 123 and 124 do not give sufficient notice -
--            Illustration (a): theft need not set out the manner; (b): cheating
--            must. So recital depth is section-dependent.
--   126.     words in a charge carry the sense the punishing law gives them -
--            which is what the Chapter 2 ladders above spell out.
--
-- Everything below is the reference row's `penal-code-general.l4`, unchanged
-- in name, field and wording, with one addition: a charge may be "read with"
-- further sections (s 109 abetment, s 511 attempt, s 120B conspiracy), so
-- `frame the charge read with` generalises `frame the charge`.

§§ `The person spoken of`

GIVEN p IS A Pronoun
GIVETH A STRING
`subject pronoun` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN he   THEN "he"
    WHEN she  THEN "she"
    WHEN they THEN "they"

GIVEN p IS A Pronoun
GIVETH A STRING
`object pronoun` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN he   THEN "him"
    WHEN she  THEN "her"
    WHEN they THEN "them"

GIVEN p IS A Pronoun
GIVETH A STRING
`possessive pronoun` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN he   THEN "his"
    WHEN she  THEN "her"
    WHEN they THEN "their"

§§ `s 123 - the charge`

@ref Criminal Procedure Code 2010 s 123 - the charge states the offence, the law and the section; s 123(5) makes it a claim that every legal condition is fulfilled
DECLARE Charge HAS
    section    IS A STRING  @desc The section of the Penal Code 1871 under which the charge is framed - the punishing section
    offence    IS A STRING  @desc The name the Code gives the offence
    `made out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether every legal condition of the offence is fulfilled on the facts given - a charge asserts that it is (CPC s 123(5))
    text       IS A STRING  @desc The charge as it is to be read to the accused, or blank if it cannot be framed on these facts
    refusal    IS A STRING  @desc Why no charge can be framed on these facts, or blank
    punishment IS A STRING  @desc The punishment the section prescribes, verbatim

§§ `The words every charge shares`

-- "You, <accused>, are charged that you, on or about <date>, at about <time>,
--  at <place>, Singapore, [together with <co-accused> and in furtherance of the
--  common intention of you all, ]"
GIVEN p IS A Particulars
GIVETH A STRING
`the opening words of the charge` p MEANS
    CONCAT "You, ", p's accused, ", are charged that you, on or about ", p's date, `the time clause` p, ", at ", p's place, ", Singapore, ", `the joint charge clause` p

GIVEN p IS A Particulars
GIVETH A STRING
`the time clause` p MEANS
    IF   p's time EQUALS ""
    THEN ""
    ELSE CONCAT ", at about ", p's time

GIVEN p IS A Particulars
GIVETH A STRING
`the joint charge clause` p MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF p's `co-accused` EQUALS "" THEN ""
        IF p's `common intention`     THEN CONCAT "together with ", p's `co-accused`, " and in furtherance of the common intention of you all, "
        OTHERWISE                          CONCAT "together with ", p's `co-accused`, ", "

-- ", and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section <N>
--  [read with section 34] of the Penal Code 1871."
GIVEN p       IS A Particulars
      section IS A STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`the closing words of the charge` p section MEANS
    CONCAT ", and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section ", section, `the section 34 rider` p, " of the Penal Code 1871."

GIVEN p IS A Particulars
GIVETH A STRING
`the section 34 rider` p MEANS
    IF   p's `common intention`
    THEN " read with section 34"
    ELSE ""

-- ", and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section <N>
--  read with section <M> [and section <M'> ...] [and section 34] of the Penal
--  Code 1871." For a charge of abetment (read with s 109), attempt (read with
--  s 511) or conspiracy (read with s 120B). With no further section it is
--  exactly `the closing words of the charge`.
GIVEN p           IS A Particulars
      section     IS A STRING
      `read with` IS A LIST OF STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`the closing words of the charge read with` p section `read with` MEANS
    CONCAT ", and you have thereby committed an offence punishable under section ", section,
           `the read-with rider` (append `read with` (`included if` (p's `common intention`) "34")),
           " of the Penal Code 1871."

GIVEN sections IS A LIST OF STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`the read-with rider` sections MEANS
    IF   null sections
    THEN ""
    ELSE CONCAT " read with section ", `joined with` " and section " sections

§§ `Framing, or refusing to frame`

-- The one place a `Charge` is built. `made out` is the verdict of the offence's
-- ladder; `body` is the offence-specific recital between the opening and the
-- closing words; `missing` names the elements that are not made out, for the
-- refusal.
GIVEN p          IS A Particulars
      section    IS A STRING
      offence    IS A STRING
      punishment IS A STRING
      `made out` IS A BOOLEAN
      body       IS A STRING
      missing    IS A LIST OF STRING
GIVETH A Charge
`frame the charge` p section offence punishment `made out` body missing MEANS
    Charge WITH
        section    IS section
        offence    IS offence
        `made out` IS `made out`
        text       IS IF `made out`
                      THEN CONCAT (`the opening words of the charge` p), body, (`the closing words of the charge` p section)
                      ELSE ""
        refusal    IS IF `made out`
                      THEN ""
                      ELSE CONCAT "A charge under section ", section, " would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of ", offence, " (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: ", `joined with` "; " missing, "."
        punishment IS punishment

-- The same, for a charge read with further sections. `section` is still the
-- section the offence is punishable under (CPC s 123(4)); the `Charge`'s
-- section field names it with its riders, e.g. "379 read with 511".
GIVEN p           IS A Particulars
      section     IS A STRING
      `read with` IS A LIST OF STRING
      offence     IS A STRING
      punishment  IS A STRING
      `made out`  IS A BOOLEAN
      body        IS A STRING
      missing     IS A LIST OF STRING
GIVETH A Charge
`frame the charge read with` p section `read with` offence punishment `made out` body missing MEANS
    Charge WITH
        section    IS IF null `read with` THEN section ELSE CONCAT section, " read with ", `joined with` " and " `read with`
        offence    IS offence
        `made out` IS `made out`
        text       IS IF `made out`
                      THEN CONCAT (`the opening words of the charge` p), body, (`the closing words of the charge read with` p section `read with`)
                      ELSE ""
        refusal    IS IF `made out`
                      THEN ""
                      ELSE CONCAT "A charge under section ", section, (`the read-with rider` `read with`), " would assert that the facts fulfil every legal condition of ", offence, " (CPC s 123(5)). On the facts given they do not: ", `joined with` "; " missing, "."
        punishment IS punishment

-- An element's name, for the refusal, unless it holds.
GIVEN holds IS A BOOLEAN
      name  IS A STRING
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`missing unless` holds name MEANS
    IF holds THEN EMPTY ELSE LIST name

-- A phrase, for the recital, if its element holds.
GIVEN holds  IS A BOOLEAN
      phrase IS A STRING
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`included if` holds phrase MEANS
    IF holds THEN LIST phrase ELSE EMPTY

GIVEN sep IS A STRING
      xs  IS A LIST OF STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`joined with` sep xs MEANS
    CONSIDER xs
    WHEN EMPTY THEN ""
    WHEN x FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        CONSIDER rest
        WHEN EMPTY THEN x
        WHEN y FOLLOWED BY more THEN CONCAT x, sep, (`joined with` sep rest)

-- "one Wong Fei Hsia, the cashier of Seiyu Departmental Store" - the person
-- against whom the offence was committed, named once with a description.
GIVEN name        IS A STRING
      description IS A STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`one` name description MEANS
    IF   description EQUALS ""
    THEN CONCAT "one ", name
    ELSE CONCAT "one ", name, ", ", description

-- The same, from a `Person`.
GIVEN p IS A Person
GIVETH A STRING
`one person` p MEANS `one` (p's name) (p's `described as`)


§ `Shared helpers, hoisted at integration`

-- Each of these was written independently by two or three chapter groups, with
-- the same name and the same body, while the groups worked in parallel. A module
-- that imported two such groups could not call the helper ("There are multiple
-- definitions for the identifier"), so the one definition now lives here.
-- The age tests read "below N years of age" as strictly under N, and
-- "above 18 years of age" as 18 or over; both are the readings every group took,
-- and the second was ruled by Meng on 2026-09-26 ("yes 18 or older").

@export Whether a child of the given age is "below 12 years of age" (ss 89, 90 and 317 of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN years IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in completed years
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `below 12 years of age` years IF years < 12

@export Whether a person of the given age is "below 14 years of age" (ss 74B, 354(2), 354A(2)(b), 375, 376, 376A, 377BB, 377BC and 377BF of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN years IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in completed years
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `below 14 years of age` years IF years < 14

@export Whether a person of the given age is "below 16 years of age" (ss 292A(2), 361 and 376A of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN years IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in completed years (for s 292A(2), the apparent age)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `below 16 years of age` years IF years < 16

@export Whether a person of the given age is "below 18 years of age" (ss 292(1B), 292(1C), 292B(2) and 305(2) of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN years IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in completed years
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `below 18 years of age` years IF years < 18

@export Whether a person of the given age is "below 21 years of age" (ss 293, 372 and 373 of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN years IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in completed years
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `below 21 years of age` years IF years < 21

@export Whether a person of the given age is "above 18 years of age" (s 87 and s 300 Exception 5 of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN years IS A NUMBER @desc The person's age in completed years
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `above 18 years of age` years IF years AT LEAST 18

@export Whether the given number of persons is "5 or more" (ss 141, 151 and 391 of the Penal Code 1871)
GIVEN persons IS A NUMBER @desc How many persons
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `5 or more persons` persons IF persons AT LEAST 5

-- ", to wit, <how>" - the particulars of the manner, when the charge gives
-- them (CPC 2010 s 125). Blank in, blank out. Two names, because two groups
-- named it differently; both are kept so that neither group's calls change.
GIVEN how IS A STRING @desc How the accused did it, or blank
GIVETH A STRING
`the to-wit clause` how MEANS
    IF   how EQUALS ""
    THEN ""
    ELSE CONCAT ", to wit, ", how

GIVEN s IS A STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`to wit` s MEANS `the to-wit clause` s
